  hard-cut fallback [3mplus[0m a "how far back is too far" threshold, which is a tunable nobody has asked for.

  Word boundary is genuinely better if you know the input is prose rendered to a human. Say so and I'll build that
  instead — it's a cheap swap either way, since the cut strategy doesn't change the option's contract.

  [1mAPI:[0m [38;5;153mtruncate[39m takes the max length as a number — [38;5;153mformat(str, { truncate: 20 })[39m.

  [1mOrdering:[0m applies after [38;5;153muppercase[39m/[38;5;153mlowercase[39m, before [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m. Case transforms don't change length, so they're
  free to run first. Prefix and suffix are decorations the caller supplies at a length they already know — silently
  eating them to stay under budget is worse than the caller subtracting 7 for their own [38;5;153m"[log] "[39m. The consequence,
  stated plainly: [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m bounds the content, so combining [38;5;153mtruncate[39m with [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m can exceed it. Tell me if you'd
  rather it bound the whole result.

  [1mBehavior:[0m
  - [38;5;153mstr.length <= maxLength[39m → returned unchanged, no ellipsis.
  - Otherwise → [38;5;153mstr.slice(0, maxLength - 3) + '...'[39m, exactly [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m chars.
  - [38;5;153mmaxLength <= 3[39m → [38;5;153m'...'.slice(0, maxLength)[39m, so [38;5;153mtruncate: 2[39m gives [38;5;153m".."[39m and [38;5;153mtruncate: 0[39m gives [38;5;153m""[39m. Keeps the
    never-exceeds guarantee at every value.
  - Guarded with [38;5;153mtypeof options.truncate === 'number'[39m rather than the truthiness check the other options use — a
    truthiness check would silently ignore [38;5;153mtruncate: 0[39m.

